Id: no-network-outside-source-adapter
Pattern: (\bnew[[:space:]]+HttpClient\(|\bIHttpClientFactory\b|\bHttpRequestMessage\b|\bHttpWebRequest\b|\bWebRequest\.|\bnew[[:space:]]+Socket\(|\bDns\.)
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template/Sources/TmdbSourceAdapter.cs :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template.Tests/AHandlerThatRefusesWhatNoTestSetUp.cs
Issue: 73

Everything that leaves this server for a third party leaves through a source
adapter. That is what makes the rate limit, the backoff, the timeout and the
terms of one source enforceable in one place instead of wherever somebody
happened to need a page of titles.

It is also what makes the test rule in #45 possible: a call that goes through
the adapter goes through the injected handler a test supplies, and a call made
anywhere else does not.

THE EXCEPTION IS THE ADAPTER'S PATH, AND IT WAS THE ADAPTER'S NAME UNTIL #387.
`*SourceAdapter.cs` matched any file in the tree whose name ended that way, so a
second place calling out of this server needed a name rather than a reason. There
is one adapter and it sits in one place, so the exception is that place. A second
source is a second adapter and a second entry here, written deliberately, and an
adapter that moves is refused by leg 6 for leaving its exception naming nothing
rather than carried along by a name that still matches.

THE SECOND EXCEPTION IS A HANDLER A TEST PUTS IN FRONT OF THE ADAPTER, AND IT IS
WHAT #45'S SECOND CONDITION NEEDED. That condition asks that every outbound call
go through one injected handler a test supplies, and the reading taken on
2026-09-04 is that the injection point is a single named client whose primary
handler is the one thing a test replaces. A substitute for it derives from
`HttpMessageHandler` and its one overridable member takes an
`HttpRequestMessage`, which this rule's pattern names, so until this line existed
no such double could be written anywhere in the tree and the suite stood in front
of the adapter's transport function instead.

It is one path rather than a shape, for the reason #387 gave when it moved both
exceptions off name suffixes: a widening by shape lets any future file somebody
names that way out of the boundary, and a widening by path is one file and reads
as one. A second handler is a second entry here, written deliberately, and leg 6
refuses an entry naming nothing.

WHAT IT DOES NOT WEAKEN. The file it excepts opens nothing. It answers what a
test set up and refuses every other address, so a call reaching it is one that
would have left the machine and did not; that is the argument written at the file
itself. The rule's reach over the plugin is unchanged: one adapter, one path.
